WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.840
Most of the time when you hear people talk about&nbsp;
big data, they’re talking about it within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.480
commercial setting about how businesses can use&nbsp;
big data in advertising or marketing strategies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.760
But, one really important place that&nbsp;
big data is also used is for consumers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.480
What’s funny about this is that, while&nbsp;
the data is there and the algorithms are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:32.560
there, and a incredibly sophisticated processing&nbsp;
is there, it’s nearly invisible. The results are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.800
so clean. They give you just a little piece of&nbsp;
information, but exactly what you need. What I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.440
want to do is show you some common applications&nbsp;
of big data for consumers that you may be using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.080
already without being aware of the sophistication&nbsp;
of the big data analysis that’s going into it.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.160
The first one is, if you have an Apple iPhone&nbsp;
or iPad, is what Siri can do. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.560
aside from saying what’s the weather like,&nbsp;
and Siri actually knows what it is you mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.040
and where you are, and what time you’re talking&nbsp;
about, it can do things like look for restaurants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.320
of a particular kind of food and see if they have&nbsp;
reservations available. It can do an enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.360
amount of things that requires the recommendation&nbsp;
of other people, awareness of your locations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.800
awareness of the changes over time of&nbsp;
what is most preferable for people.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:23.600
The next one, you might be familiar&nbsp;
with recommendation engines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.320
This is an idea of software that is able to make&nbsp;
a specific suggestion to you. Here’s a Spotify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.720
account, and Spotify knows what you listen to&nbsp;
when you’re on Spotify, and what you listen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.200
to all the way through, what you add to your&nbsp;
playlist, what you skip through, and it’s able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.520
to make specific suggestions to help you find new&nbsp;
artists that you wouldn’t otherwise know about.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.080
Similarly, Amazon.com makes&nbsp;
recommendations for books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.200
For instance, here’s a book, Principles&nbsp;
of Big Data, by Jules Berman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.120
and if you scroll down you’ll see that they&nbsp;
have a list of other books that are recommended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.120
This is generated by Amazon’s recommendation&nbsp;
engine, and you see several other books on big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.240
data, and in fact, it’s a great list.&nbsp;
It’s the same general principle here.&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.440
A lot of people use Netflix to get movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.760
Netflix makes specific suggestions&nbsp;
for other movies you might like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.360
What’s interesting is that a few years ago they&nbsp;
had a major contest called the Netflix Prize where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.760
they wanted to see if anybody could improve the&nbsp;
accuracy of their predictions, meaning would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:36.240
actually like this. If they could improve those&nbsp;
predictions by 10%, it was a million dollar prize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.600
and it was incredibly sophisticated analysis that&nbsp;
went into this, but the end result again is a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.640
simple thing, you get recommended a hand full of&nbsp;
movies, and usually you pick one and you like it.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.040
So for consumers, big data plays an enormous&nbsp;
role in providing valuable services, but again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.680
with the irony that it operates invisibly by&nbsp;
taking a huge amount of information from several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.840
different sources and distilling it into just&nbsp;
two or three things that give you what you need.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.760
We saw that big data can provide important&nbsp;
conveniences and functionality for consumers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.160
but for the business world, big data is&nbsp;
revolutionizing the way people do commerce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.160
We will look at a few particular places where&nbsp;
big data has proven to be particularly useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.200
or unusual and interesting. The first thing&nbsp;
we’re going to do is look at the place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:31.840
most people have encountered big data in commerce,&nbsp;
and that’s in the results for Google ad searches.&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.320
Whenever you search for something on Google or&nbsp;
any other search engine, you type in your term.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.720
You’re going to get the results that you&nbsp;
want, but you’re also going to get ads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.080
You see here, for instance, on the top&nbsp;
when you’re searching for big data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.000
There are three ads on the top, and there&nbsp;
is a series of ads down the right side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.600
Those ads are not placed at random. They’re&nbsp;
placed there because they are based first on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.840
the thing that you’re currently searching for,&nbsp;
but also based on what Google knows about you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.360
You can see in the top right that someone has&nbsp;
logged in to his account, so Google is drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.080
on all of the things that he has searched for, and&nbsp;
the other information it has about him, to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.280
place ads that it thinks he would be most likely&nbsp;
to respond to. That’s something it gets by having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.160
a very large amount of data available, to tailor&nbsp;
things to be most applicable to the consumer.&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.960
Another interesting place is what’s called&nbsp;
predictive marketing. This is when big data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.040
is used to help decide who the audience would&nbsp;
be for something before they actually get there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.280
This is trying to predict, for&nbsp;
example, major life events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.400
like for instance graduating, or getting&nbsp;
married, or getting a new job, or having a child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.040
or any number of events that are often associated&nbsp;
with a whole series of commercial transactions.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.800
To do this, these companies can look at&nbsp;
consumer behavior. They can look at how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.640
often you log onto their website. They can&nbsp;
look at what credit cards you use. They can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.480
look at how often you look at particular&nbsp;
items before moving on to something else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.640
They can look at whether you’ve applied&nbsp;
for an account at their organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.960
They can make a huge amount of information that&nbsp;
they already have available to them. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.800
they can use demographic information. This can&nbsp;
include things like your age, your marital status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.360
the number of children you have, your home&nbsp;
address, how far you live from their store, your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:26.640
estimated salary, if you’ve moved recently, what&nbsp;
credit cards you have, what websites you visit.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.480
All of this information is potentially available&nbsp;
in one form or another, to the company that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.840
trying to make these predictions. Similarly,&nbsp;
they can rely on additional purchased data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.360
It’s possible for the company to get information&nbsp;
about your ethnicity, your job history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.600
your magazine subscriptions, whether you’ve&nbsp;
declared bankruptcy or been divorced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.840
whether you’ve attended college, the kind&nbsp;
of things you talk about online, and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.080
There’s an enormous amount of information here&nbsp;
that again is potentially available. Using this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.120
kind of information, it is possible for a company&nbsp;
to predict that you’re about to buy a new house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.640
and that when you buy a new house you&nbsp;
make an enormous number of purchases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:09.920
and so they can link in to you before you&nbsp;
start making the commitments to those.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.440
Another place we want to talk about&nbsp;
big data is trying to predict trends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.120
One of the really fascinating places for&nbsp;
this is in fashion. The company Editd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.520
has actually received awards for their use&nbsp;
of big data in predicting fashion trends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.400
So they can actually tell retailers what&nbsp;
the most popular colors and styles and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:34.560
brands are going to be, when they’re going to&nbsp;
be popular, and they can help them price them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.160
Obviously, this kind of information is enormously&nbsp;
important to the companies that are going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.560
selling these products, and Editd is able to&nbsp;
do this through their reliance on big data.&nbsp;

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:52.320
Finally, one of the interesting use of big data&nbsp;
in commerce is for fraud detection. It turns out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.400
that fraud is an enormous industry, that online&nbsp;
retailers lose about $3.5 billion each year to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.600
online fraud, and insurance fraud, not counting&nbsp;
health insurance, is estimated to be more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:10.400
$40 billion per year. So fraud is a big issue.&nbsp;
It turns out that there’s a number of things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.840
that companies can do to lessen the prevalence&nbsp;
of fraud, especially through online transactions.&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.920
&nbsp;
They can look at the point of sale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.520
That specifically means, how are you&nbsp;
making the purchase? Are you online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:29.920
and what website are you using? They can use&nbsp;
geolocation. Where are you physically located&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:36.720
in the world? They can look at the IP address.&nbsp;
What computer are you using to access the website.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.480
They can look at the log in time. Are you&nbsp;
somehow making a purchase at 4:00 a.m.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.200
when you’ve never before done anything&nbsp;
after 11:00 p.m. . Interestingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:52.960
they can also look at things like biometrics. For&nbsp;
instance, the way that people move their mouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.840
or the time they take between pressing keys on the&nbsp;
computer, are distinctive measurements of people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:03.200
A lot of this was made possible because of the&nbsp;
particular details and the patterns that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.480
have in their extraordinarily large data set, let&nbsp;
them recognize these anomalies as potential fraud.

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.960
We have looked at the role that big&nbsp;
data can play in individual people’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:19.360
lives as well as in businesses. We&nbsp;
also want to look very quickly at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.520
how big data has been revolutionizing&nbsp;
aspects of scholarship and research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:29.120
There are a few interesting examples of where&nbsp;
big data has influenced scientific progress.&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:35.120
The first one we want to look at is Google flu&nbsp;
trends where they were able to find that search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.440
patterns for flu related words were actually&nbsp;
able to identify outbreaks of the flu in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:43.760
United States much faster than the research&nbsp;
that the Center for Disease Control could do.&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.320
Similarly, a more recent project found that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.840
Wikipedia searches could identify&nbsp;
them with even greater accuracy.&nbsp;

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:02.480
Psychological research has also been influenced&nbsp;
through big data. In 2013, a paper published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:07.360
about personalities in the United States was&nbsp;
able to identify clusters of personalities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.360
in regions where we have the mid-western friendly&nbsp;
and conventional region[sPause sec=0.4 ePause],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.840
the western relaxed and creative region[sPause&nbsp;
sec=0.4 ePause], and a northeastern and somewhat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:19.360
southern temperamental and uninhibited&nbsp;
region[sPause sec=0.4 ePause]. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:24.080
not based on simply how do you feel about the&nbsp;
places, but this is published in a journal from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.960
the American Psychological Association, a&nbsp;
very high quality psychological research.&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:36.240
Similarly, another group of researchers created an&nbsp;
application on Facebook that used a scientifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.760
valid measure of personality. They collected&nbsp;
data from several hundred thousand respondents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:47.440
And by combining those with the patterns of&nbsp;
likes, that each of those people had on Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:51.280
to give a surprisingly accurate evaluation&nbsp;
of what your personality would be.&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:58.880
Finally, the Google Books project. For the last&nbsp;
few years, Google has been scanning books that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:04.240
were published over the last few hundred years.&nbsp;
They currently have over 30 million books that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.640
they have scanned, they make them digitally&nbsp;
accessible, and that allows people in Digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:15.200
Humanitites to look at the changes in word usage&nbsp;
over time. There’s some interesting things. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.619
instance, we have the last 208 years of the&nbsp;
prevalence of the word, ‘math’[sPause sec=0.4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.619 --> 00:10:24.560
ePause], ‘arithmetic’[sPause sec=0.4 ePause],&nbsp;
and ‘algebra’. The term ‘arithmetic’ shows a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:29.200
strong spike in the 20s and 30s, but it decreases&nbsp;
over time [sPause sec=0.4 ePause],whereas the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:34.080
word ‘math’ has increased over the last 50 to&nbsp;
60 years, with a peak right around year 2000.

